1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,560
This is Inwood Hill Park at the
northern tip of Manhattan.

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:06,080
A bronze plaque sits here on a
rock.

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,759
It commemorates the purchase of
Manhattan for trinkets and beads

4
00:00:09,759 --> 00:00:12,160
worth about sixty guilders.

5
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:15,000
The city's official record
begins with a warning.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,460
The plaque states the story

7
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:18,440
happened according to legend,

8
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:19,879
the only reason this legend

9
00:00:19,879 --> 00:00:21,559
could be manufactured is because

10
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:22,839
the archival evidence is

11
00:00:22,839 --> 00:00:23,800
missing.

12
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,440
To understand why we have no
historical sources about the

13
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:30,440
founding of New York, we must go
back to eighteen twenty one.

14
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,320
In eighteen twenty one, the
government of the Netherlands

15
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,240
had a storage problem.

16
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,399
They decided to clear out their
colonial archives.

17
00:00:38,399 --> 00:00:39,920
They sold about eighty thousand

18
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:41,840
pounds of records to scrap

19
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,320
paper dealers.

20
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:44,840
These documents covered the

21
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:46,740
colonial past before seventeen

22
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:47,600
hundred.

23
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:51,479
This act erased the primary
evidence of the city's birth.

24
00:00:51,479 --> 00:00:55,520
Only one document mentioning the
purchase of Manhattan survived.

25
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,600
It is called the Schagen Letter.

26
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,600
It is not a deed or a contract.

27
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:03,770
It is a cargo report for company
directors, most of the letter

28
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:05,930
inventories animal pelts.

29
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:08,049
It lists seven thousand

30
00:01:08,049 --> 00:01:09,609
two hundred and forty six beaver

31
00:01:09,609 --> 00:01:10,450
skins.

32
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:14,129
It also lists almost one hundred
and eighty otter skins.

33
00:01:14,129 --> 00:01:17,329
One sentence is buried beneath
the animal cargo.

34
00:01:17,329 --> 00:01:19,329
It states the settlers purchased

35
00:01:19,329 --> 00:01:21,129
the island Manhattan, from the

36
00:01:21,129 --> 00:01:23,069
Indians for the value of sixty

37
00:01:23,069 --> 00:01:24,250
guilders.

38
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,530
The document does not name the
sellers.

39
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:30,530
It also doesn't include how the
purchase was made.

40
00:01:30,530 --> 00:01:32,489
The sixteen twenty six record is

41
00:01:32,489 --> 00:01:34,250
a single sentence in a ledger of

42
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:35,650
dead beavers.

43
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:39,010
Every other detail was
engineered later.

44
00:01:39,010 --> 00:01:42,370
The legend claims the Dutch
purchased Manhattan for exactly

45
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:44,090
twenty four dollars.

46
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:45,890
This figure appears in textbooks

47
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:46,969
and lives in the collective

48
00:01:46,969 --> 00:01:48,209
memory.

49
00:01:48,209 --> 00:01:49,489
But the twenty four dollars

50
00:01:49,489 --> 00:01:51,010
figure is a nineteenth century

51
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:52,290
invention.

52
00:01:52,290 --> 00:01:54,170
A journalist named Edmund Bailey

53
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:56,129
O'Callaghan manufactured his

54
00:01:56,129 --> 00:01:58,750
conversion in eighteen forty

55
00:01:58,750 --> 00:01:59,290
four.

56
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:03,739
He found the Schagen letter and
saw the sixty guilder price tag.

57
00:02:03,739 --> 00:02:07,379
O'Callahan used the exchange
rate of his own time to create a

58
00:02:07,379 --> 00:02:09,580
twenty four dollars figure.

59
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,979
This was a mathematical fallacy.

60
00:02:11,979 --> 00:02:13,659
Converting a sixteen twenty six

61
00:02:13,659 --> 00:02:15,520
transaction using eighteen forty

62
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:16,860
four exchange rates is

63
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:18,819
meaningless, but ignores the

64
00:02:18,819 --> 00:02:20,659
shifting value of currency over

65
00:02:20,659 --> 00:02:22,340
two centuries.

66
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:25,360
The number tells us what a
journalist wanted his readers to

67
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,539
see in eighteen forty four.

68
00:02:27,539 --> 00:02:28,379
That tells us nothing.

69
00:02:28,379 --> 00:02:30,780
About sixteen twenty six.

70
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:33,180
Historian Russell Shorto notes

71
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:34,539
that sixty guilders was a

72
00:02:34,539 --> 00:02:35,879
significant sum in sixteen

73
00:02:35,879 --> 00:02:37,300
twenty six.

74
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:40,680
The soldier for the West India
Company earned about one hundred

75
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,060
guilders a year.

76
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:44,080
Manhattan cost more than half a

77
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,580
year's salary for a colonial

78
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:47,780
employee in sixteen thirty

79
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:48,340
eight.

80
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:52,219
One hundred acres on Long Island
sold between Dutch colonists for

81
00:02:52,219 --> 00:02:54,180
fifty two guilders.

82
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:57,219
Wilderness land was simply
cheap.

83
00:02:57,219 --> 00:03:00,909
O'Callahan completely ignored
two hundred years of inflation.

84
00:03:00,909 --> 00:03:05,270
To make the price sound absurd,
that eighteen forty four number

85
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:09,110
is still the figure most people
carry in their heads today.

86
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:10,949
I hope I've demonstrated why the

87
00:03:10,949 --> 00:03:12,310
twenty four dollars number is

88
00:03:12,310 --> 00:03:13,229
wrong.

89
00:03:13,229 --> 00:03:16,430
Now let's look at the forged
physical evidence.

90
00:03:16,430 --> 00:03:20,289
The legend claims Peter Minuit
sealed the deal by handing over

91
00:03:20,289 --> 00:03:22,389
a chest of glass beads.

92
00:03:22,389 --> 00:03:26,229
The sixteen twenty six letter
does not mention beads.

93
00:03:26,229 --> 00:03:29,310
It provides no inventory of
goods at all.

94
00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:32,830
The visual of the glass beads
traces to a single painting from

95
00:03:32,830 --> 00:03:34,909
eighteen fifty three.

96
00:03:34,909 --> 00:03:38,590
The artist of that painting was
a man named William Ranney.

97
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:42,349
Ranney manufactured the visual
iconography of the purchase two

98
00:03:42,349 --> 00:03:44,069
centuries after the event.

99
00:03:44,069 --> 00:03:47,229
He did this through mechanical
plagiarism.

100
00:03:47,229 --> 00:03:50,430
Ranney copied the composition of
a famous seventeen seventy one

101
00:03:50,430 --> 00:03:52,830
painting by Benjamin West.

102
00:03:52,830 --> 00:03:54,990
West's painting depicted William

103
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:56,590
Penn's treaty with the Indians

104
00:03:56,590 --> 00:03:58,509
in Pennsylvania.

105
00:03:58,509 --> 00:04:02,039
Ranee stole specific visual
elements from west.

106
00:04:02,039 --> 00:04:05,520
The large elm tree on the right
side of the canvas is identical.

107
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:08,800
The kneeling figure examining
the goods is identical.

108
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:13,039
The open chest in the center of
the group is identical.

109
00:04:13,039 --> 00:04:14,719
Ranney took a famous painting of

110
00:04:14,719 --> 00:04:16,800
Pennsylvania and rebranded it as

111
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:17,839
Manhattan.

112
00:04:17,839 --> 00:04:21,319
He added the chest of beads to
the center of the frame.

113
00:04:21,319 --> 00:04:24,920
In eighteen seventy five, a
historian named Martha Lamb

114
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:27,040
wrote this piece into history.

115
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:29,720
She was not citing a primary
source.

116
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:31,040
She was simply describing

117
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:32,279
William Ranney's fictional

118
00:04:32,279 --> 00:04:34,000
painting.

119
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,439
Another popular legend claims
the Canarsee tribe pulled off a

120
00:04:37,439 --> 00:04:39,199
real estate scam.

121
00:04:39,199 --> 00:04:42,879
The story says the Canarsee
actually lived in Brooklyn.

122
00:04:42,879 --> 00:04:44,959
They allegedly sold Manhattan to

123
00:04:44,959 --> 00:04:46,519
the Dutch despite not owning the

124
00:04:46,519 --> 00:04:48,199
ground themselves.

125
00:04:48,199 --> 00:04:50,040
The sixteen twenty six Schagen

126
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:51,819
letter does not name a specific

127
00:04:51,819 --> 00:04:53,279
tribe.

128
00:04:53,279 --> 00:04:56,079
It refers only to the Indians.

129
00:04:56,079 --> 00:05:00,569
No seventeenth century document
identifies the settlers by name.

130
00:05:00,569 --> 00:05:03,569
Anthropologist Robert Grumet
confirms the identity of the

131
00:05:03,569 --> 00:05:07,610
Native Americans who sold the
island as completely unknown.

132
00:05:07,610 --> 00:05:11,569
The Canarsie trickster story is
a modern fabrication.

133
00:05:11,569 --> 00:05:13,129
Historians trace the earliest

134
00:05:13,129 --> 00:05:14,509
account of it to a nineteen

135
00:05:14,509 --> 00:05:16,910
fifty nine article by Nathaniel

136
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:18,050
Benchley.

137
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:20,810
Benchley wrote a story for a
magazine article.

138
00:05:20,810 --> 00:05:24,209
He provided no archival evidence
for his claim.

139
00:05:24,209 --> 00:05:26,490
It is a mid-20th century

140
00:05:26,490 --> 00:05:28,290
invention designed to make a

141
00:05:28,290 --> 00:05:29,930
land grab look like a harmless

142
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:31,250
prank.

143
00:05:31,250 --> 00:05:34,329
The true inhabitants of the
region were the Lenape.

144
00:05:34,329 --> 00:05:35,610
They had lived on the lands that

145
00:05:35,610 --> 00:05:37,129
would become New York City for

146
00:05:37,129 --> 00:05:39,389
thousands of years when the

147
00:05:39,389 --> 00:05:40,370
Dutch arrived.

148
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:41,730
They attempted to secure this

149
00:05:41,730 --> 00:05:43,370
territory using European

150
00:05:43,370 --> 00:05:44,730
contracts.

151
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:48,250
Lenape didn't recognize the
earth as private property.

152
00:05:48,250 --> 00:05:49,649
This conflict between the Dutch

153
00:05:49,649 --> 00:05:51,250
and Lenape was a complete

154
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:52,810
mechanical failure of legal

155
00:05:52,810 --> 00:05:53,850
systems.

156
00:05:53,850 --> 00:05:58,050
The two sides were operating
under entirely different rules.

157
00:05:58,050 --> 00:06:02,259
The Dutch followed a statutory
system called fee simple.

158
00:06:02,259 --> 00:06:04,120
This is the European concept of

159
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:05,980
absolute permanent private

160
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:07,019
property.

161
00:06:07,019 --> 00:06:09,939
Under this system, land was a
commodity.

162
00:06:09,939 --> 00:06:12,740
You buy the land and you own it
forever.

163
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:13,899
The Dutch believed they were

164
00:06:13,899 --> 00:06:15,519
acquiring exclusive rights to

165
00:06:15,519 --> 00:06:16,620
the island.

166
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:19,459
They expected the original
inhabitants to leave.

167
00:06:19,459 --> 00:06:21,019
The Lenape operated under

168
00:06:21,019 --> 00:06:22,240
customary system called

169
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:23,259
usufruct.

170
00:06:23,259 --> 00:06:24,860
This system grants the right to

171
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:27,019
use the land only as long as it

172
00:06:27,019 --> 00:06:29,259
is actively utilized to the

173
00:06:29,259 --> 00:06:29,939
Lenape.

174
00:06:29,939 --> 00:06:33,379
The transaction was a temporary
shared use alliance.

175
00:06:33,379 --> 00:06:35,540
They saw the Dutch as new
roommates.

176
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:39,420
They did not see them as the
permanent new owners.

177
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:43,100
We have direct testimony
explaining this perspective.

178
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:44,540
Lenape elder.

179
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:49,379
He stated that the land was like
air or sunlight.

180
00:06:49,379 --> 00:06:52,019
It was necessary for survival.

181
00:06:52,019 --> 00:06:55,620
It could not be permanently sold
away from the community.

182
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:58,540
The Lenape thought they were
simply giving the settlers land

183
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:00,230
to live on for a while.

184
00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:03,949
Robert Grumet explains exactly
why this collision happens.

185
00:07:03,949 --> 00:07:07,769
They were facing devastating new
diseases and threats from rival

186
00:07:07,769 --> 00:07:09,350
tribes like the Mohawk.

187
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:11,029
They accepted metal tools and

188
00:07:11,029 --> 00:07:12,230
goods to secure powerful

189
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:13,870
military allies.

190
00:07:13,870 --> 00:07:15,589
They were not selling the
ground.

191
00:07:15,589 --> 00:07:18,750
They were building a strategic
alliance to survive.

192
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:22,629
Only the Dutch viewed the deal
as a final permanent sale.

193
00:07:22,629 --> 00:07:24,069
The history of Manhattan is

194
00:07:24,069 --> 00:07:25,149
built on these mechanical

195
00:07:25,149 --> 00:07:27,110
misunderstandings and missing

196
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:28,069
records.

197
00:07:28,069 --> 00:07:31,069
If you value this forensic
approach to history, please

198
00:07:31,069 --> 00:07:32,750
subscribe to my channel.

199
00:07:32,750 --> 00:07:37,189
I try to rely on primary sources
to dismantle historical legends.

200
00:07:37,189 --> 00:07:39,949
You might be asking why people
created this myth.

201
00:07:39,949 --> 00:07:43,829
These myths were built by
commercial elites, like a lawyer

202
00:07:43,829 --> 00:07:45,949
named Simeon DeWitt Bloodgood.

203
00:07:45,949 --> 00:07:49,310
In eighteen forty four,
Bloodgood delivered a speech at

204
00:07:49,310 --> 00:07:51,269
the New York Historical Society.

205
00:07:51,269 --> 00:07:55,269
He argued that the Manhattan
purchase placed New York on a

206
00:07:55,269 --> 00:07:58,790
high ground of justice as the
land of William Penn.

207
00:07:58,790 --> 00:08:01,519
This speech reveals their exact
motive.

208
00:08:01,519 --> 00:08:05,639
The New York elite felt inferior
to New England and Pennsylvania.

209
00:08:05,639 --> 00:08:08,319
They desperately needed an
honest business deal to serve as

210
00:08:08,319 --> 00:08:10,360
a noble origin story.

211
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:11,399
They wanted a peaceful

212
00:08:11,399 --> 00:08:13,040
foundation to rival William

213
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:14,360
Penn's famous treaty in

214
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:15,879
Pennsylvania.

215
00:08:15,879 --> 00:08:18,720
But when they looked for
evidence, they found the archive

216
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:20,319
almost completely empty.

217
00:08:20,319 --> 00:08:21,519
The original documents had been

218
00:08:21,519 --> 00:08:23,079
sold for scrap paper in eighteen

219
00:08:23,079 --> 00:08:24,560
twenty one.

220
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:27,360
Dismissing archive provided a
perfect cover.

221
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,040
These men acted as tactical

222
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,519
engineers of a new civic

223
00:08:30,519 --> 00:08:31,959
identity.

224
00:08:31,959 --> 00:08:33,759
They systematically projected

225
00:08:33,759 --> 00:08:35,379
their own nineteenth century

226
00:08:35,379 --> 00:08:37,399
capitalist ideas backward onto

227
00:08:37,399 --> 00:08:38,720
that void.

228
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:39,679
They needed to prove that

229
00:08:39,679 --> 00:08:40,919
Manhattan was not taken by

230
00:08:40,919 --> 00:08:41,799
force.

231
00:08:41,799 --> 00:08:44,759
They needed to prove it was
bought fairly.

232
00:08:44,759 --> 00:08:47,919
They manufactured a modern
business transaction to create

233
00:08:47,919 --> 00:08:52,200
moral and legal legitimacy for
their rapidly growing city.

234
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:53,600
This is exactly why William

235
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:55,399
Ranney plagiarized the painting

236
00:08:55,399 --> 00:08:57,519
of Penn's Treaty, and why the

237
00:08:57,519 --> 00:08:58,759
twenty four dollars and the

238
00:08:58,759 --> 00:09:00,830
glass beads were invented to

239
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:02,409
fill this specific ideological

240
00:09:02,409 --> 00:09:03,490
need.

241
00:09:03,490 --> 00:09:06,490
I recognized this pattern of
retrofitting history.

242
00:09:06,490 --> 00:09:09,690
I am Belgian and I currently
live in South America.

243
00:09:09,690 --> 00:09:11,250
In Belgium, the brutal

244
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:12,730
exploitation of the Congo was

245
00:09:12,730 --> 00:09:13,450
rewritten.

246
00:09:13,450 --> 00:09:15,090
It was covered up with a story

247
00:09:15,090 --> 00:09:16,970
about a noble civilizing

248
00:09:16,970 --> 00:09:17,929
mission.

249
00:09:17,929 --> 00:09:19,409
The Spanish repackaged the

250
00:09:19,409 --> 00:09:21,049
extreme violence of the South

251
00:09:21,049 --> 00:09:23,049
American conquest as a heroic

252
00:09:23,049 --> 00:09:24,490
discovery.

253
00:09:24,490 --> 00:09:27,649
The mechanism of erasure is
almost exactly the same.

254
00:09:27,649 --> 00:09:30,649
Nations do not like boring or
ugly history.

255
00:09:30,649 --> 00:09:34,929
Real history is usually just
dry, boring bureaucracy.

256
00:09:34,929 --> 00:09:36,610
It is a record of incompatible

257
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:37,970
legal systems and forgotten

258
00:09:37,970 --> 00:09:39,490
paperwork.

259
00:09:39,490 --> 00:09:42,210
People would much rather print a
good story.

260
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:44,370
In sixteen twenty six, the story

261
00:09:44,370 --> 00:09:46,049
was a single line of ink about

262
00:09:46,049 --> 00:09:48,610
dead beavers in eighteen twenty

263
00:09:48,610 --> 00:09:49,210
one.

264
00:09:49,210 --> 00:09:50,850
The archives were sold for scrap

265
00:09:50,850 --> 00:09:53,250
paper in the centuries since,

266
00:09:53,250 --> 00:09:54,850
people filled that silence with

267
00:09:54,850 --> 00:09:57,409
twenty four dollars glass beads

268
00:09:57,409 --> 00:09:58,730
and legends.
